methods:
\paragraph{Candidate reuse.}
Twenty additional cost instances per family are generated using the same distributions and fixed constraints. Each is evaluated on the three hardware sample pools used for independent evaluation of FLP20 in the main study and TSP25/VRP20 with longer annealing schedules. All four preparations are included. Rescoring changes neither the samples nor their feasibility. The 60 combinations of sample pool and cost instance per method share observations and do not constitute independent hardware replications. A uniform distribution over feasible strings supplies a classical reference at the same number of feasible draws.

\paragraph{Parameter adaptation.}
Two further cost instances per family test FLP20, TSP16, and VRP20. Adaptation begins from the highest-fidelity two-layer fit to the first instance of the main study in each family, selected from three initializations. Targets are complete 12-step annealing states with $T_{\mathrm{ann}}=2$. Circuit architecture, physical patch, variable meanings, constraints, penalties, and normalization rules are fixed. Adaptation and fitting without prior parameters each use three initializations and 40 objective/gradient evaluations per initialization. These begin from prior parameters and zero angles, respectively, followed by two starts with Gaussian perturbations of standard deviation 0.02 and 0.04. Circuits are selected by fidelity to the exact source alone, without preliminary hardware measurements. The fitting cost for the initial objective is additional to the 120 evaluations per new objective.

Circuits with unchanged parameters, adapted parameters, and reinitialized fits are executed in two independently randomized blocks of 8,192 shots per method and instance on Nighthawk. All have CZ depth 16 and use the same error-suppression settings as the main study. The 36 block-stratified tests compare adaptation with each reference for feasible, near-optimal, and optimal events, with one joint Holm correction.

reuse_results:
\paragraph{Candidate reuse under changed costs.}
\label{sec:candidate-reuse}
For a sampled pool $S$ and a new objective $C'$, candidate reuse returns
\begin{equation}
\hat{x}(C';S)\in\arg\min_{x\in S\cap\mathcal F} C'(x),
\label{eq:candidate-reuse}
\end{equation}
when $S\cap\mathcal F$ is nonempty. Because the constraints and variable meanings are fixed, the observed feasible fraction is unchanged. The operation can select a different assignment or route, but cannot recover an optimum absent from the pool.

Selected AQC pools contain an optimum for the new objective in 7, 37, and 57 of the 60 combinations for FLP20, TSP25, and VRP20, respectively (Table~\ref{tab:candidate-reuse}). They contain a decision within 5\% of the new optimum in 7, 57, and 60 combinations. The shallow control contains a TSP optimum more often than selected AQC, consistent with its larger observed candidate pools. For FLP, the selected pools contain only 67--83 distinct feasible strings out of 680, and the median gap of the best candidate under new costs is 29.1\%. These pools provide feasible candidates under revised costs. Their coverage determines the best attainable objective value.

For uniform classical sampling from the feasible set, the expected probabilities of obtaining at least one optimum are 11.7\%, 64.9\%, and 99.3\% at the same numbers of feasible draws. The observed proportions for selected AQC are 11.7\%, 61.7\%, and 95.0\%. This comparison provides no evidence of superior coverage relative to classical feasible sampling. It does not compare equal total hardware shots or runtime.

adaptation_results:
\paragraph{Parameter adaptation to changed costs.}
\label{sec:cost-adaptation}
The six additional instances evaluate parameter adaptation separately from candidate reuse. With 120 additional fitting evaluations per method, adapted circuits have higher fidelity to the exact source than reinitialized fits in every case (Table~\ref{tab:cost-adaptation}). The improvement over fitting without prior parameters is 0.088--0.176 in squared fidelity. Relative to unchanged parameters, however, the gain is only 0.00007--0.00052. The new and original source states have squared overlaps of 0.99964--0.99997, so prior parameters already approximate most of the new target structure.

Hardware sampling shows no significant increase or decrease after correction for any event relative to either reference. TSP produces optimal routes with all three methods, FLP yields none, and VRP optimum counts are at most one per preparation. These six cases show the fitting benefit of initializing from prior parameters for closely related costs.

